== 7. Vain Thoughts in Duty

#strong[The seventh #emph[device] that Satan has to keep souls from holy and heavenly services, is,]

By casting in a multitude of vain thoughts while the soul is seeking God, or waiting on God. By this device he has cooled some men’s spirits in heavenly services, and drawn many precious#footnote[#emph[Vellem servire Domine sed cogitationes non patiuntur:] Lord, how gladly I would serve you, and vain thoughts will not allow me.] souls away (at least for a time) from religious exercises. I have no heart to hear, no heart to pray, no delight in reading, nor in the company of the saints, etc. Satan so dogs and follows my soul, and is always casting in such a multitude of vain thoughts concerning God, the world, and my own soul, etc., that I even tremble to think of waiting on God in any religious service. Oh, the vain thoughts that Satan casts in so sour my soul, and so grieve, vex, perplex, and distract my soul, that they even make me weary of holy duties, yes, of my very life! Oh, I cannot be so raised and enraptured, so heated and melted, so revived and set free, so comforted and refreshed as I should be, as I might be, and as I would be in religious services, because of the multitude of vain thoughts that Satan is injecting, or casting into my soul, etc.

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first #emph[remedy] against this device#footnote[When Pompey could not keep his soldiers in the camp by persuasion, he threw himself down full length in the narrow passage that led out of it, and then told them, “Go if you#super[+] will, but you#super[+] must first trample on your#super[+] general”; and the thought of this overcame them. You#super[+] are wise, and know how to apply it to the point in hand.] of Satan is to have your#super[+] hearts strongly moved by the greatness, holiness, majesty, and glory of the God before whom you#super[+] stand, and with whom your#super[+] souls commune in religious services. Oh, let your#super[+] souls be greatly moved by the presence, purity, and majesty of the God before whom you stand! A man would be afraid of playing with a feather when he is speaking to a king. Ah, when men have poor, low, light, slight, etc. thoughts of God in their drawing near to God, they tempt the devil to stir himself, and to cast in a multitude of vain thoughts to disturb and distract the soul in its waiting on God. Nothing will contribute so much to keeping out vain thoughts as to look on God as an omniscient God, an omnipresent God, an omnipotent God, a God full of all glorious perfections, a God whose majesty, purity, and glory will not allow him to look on the least iniquity. The reason the blessed saints and glorious angels in heaven do not have so much as one vain thought is that they are greatly moved by the greatness, holiness, majesty, purity, and glory of God.

The second #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to be determined in religious services, notwithstanding all the wandering thoughts the soul#footnote[It is a rule in the civil law (#emph[nec videtur actum, siquid supersit quod agatur]): nothing seems to be done if anything remains to be done.] is troubled with. It will be a sweet help against them for the soul to be resolute in waiting on God, whether it is troubled with vain thoughts or not, and to say, “Well, I will go on praying, and go on hearing, and go on meditating, and go on keeping fellowship with the saints.” Many precious souls can say from experience that when their souls have been determined in their waiting on God, Satan has left them, and has not been so busy vexing their souls with vain thoughts. Satan may perceive that all#footnote[#emph[Si dixisti sufficit periisti:] “If once you say, ‘It is enough,’ you are undone,” says Augustine.] the trifling vain thoughts he casts into the soul only vex the soul into greater diligence, carefulness, watchfulness, and determination in holy and heavenly services, and that the soul loses nothing of its zeal, piety, and devotion, but doubles its care, diligence, and earnestness. When he does, he often stops throwing in his trifles and vain thoughts, as he stopped tempting Christ when Christ was determined in resisting his temptations.

The third #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider that the vain and trifling thoughts that are cast#footnote[It is not Satan’s casting in of vain thoughts that can keep mercy from the soul, or undo the soul, but the lodging and cherishing of vain thoughts. “O Jerusalem, … how long will vain thoughts lodge within you?” (Jer 4:14); in Hebrew, “in the midst of you.” They pass through the best hearts; they are lodged and cherished only in the worst hearts.] into our souls when we are waiting on God in this or that religious service are not sins upon our souls, if they are not cherished and indulged, but abhorred, resisted, and disowned. They may be troubles to our minds, but they will not be put to our account, nor keep mercies and blessings from being enjoyed by us. Suppose a soul can in uprightness look God in the face and say, “Lord, when I draw near to you, a world of vain thoughts crowds in on me, disturbing my soul, weakening my faith, and lessening my comfort and spiritual strength. Oh, these are my clog, my burden, my torment, my hell! Oh, execute justice on these! Free me from these, so that I may serve you with more freedom, singleness, spirituality, and sweetness of spirit.” Then these thoughts may vex that soul, but they will not harm that soul, nor keep a blessing from that soul. If vain thoughts that are resisted and lamented could stop the current of mercy and make a soul unhappy, there would be no one on earth who would ever taste mercy, or be everlastingly happy, etc.

The fourth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly that watching against sinful thoughts, resisting sinful thoughts, lamenting and weeping over sinful thoughts, carries#footnote[Ps 139:23. Thoughts are the firstborn, the blossoms of the soul, the beginning of our strength, whether for good or evil, and they are the greatest evidence for or against a man that there can be.] with it the sweetest and strongest evidence of the truth and power of grace, and of the sincerity of your#super[+] hearts, and is the readiest and surest way to be rid of them. Many low and carnal considerations may move men to watch their words, their lives, their actions, such as the hope of gain, or the wish to please friends or to get a name in the world, and many other such considerations. Oh, but to watch our thoughts, to weep and lament over them, etc., must come from some noble, spiritual, and inward principle, such as love to God, a holy fear of God, a holy care and delight to please the Lord, etc. The schoolmen rightly observe that outward sins are of greater#footnote[Majoris infamiae. Majoris reatus.] infamy, but inward heart sins are of greater guilt, as we see in the devils. Nothing shows a man to have been thoroughly and genuinely worked upon so much as his having his thoughts#footnote[2 Cor 10:4, 5.] brought into obedience, as the apostle says. Grace has grown to a very great height in the soul where it prevails to the subduing of the vain thoughts that walk up and down in the soul. Well, though you#super[+] cannot be#footnote[Ps 139:2. Isa 59:7 ch. 66:18. Matt 9:4 ch. 12:25. Zeno, a wise heathen, affirmed that God beholds even the thoughts. Matt 15:15, 16, 17, 18, 19.] rid of them, yet resist and oppose their first risings. When sinful thoughts arise, then think this way: The Lord takes notice of these thoughts; he knows them “from afar,” as the psalmist says. He knew Herod’s bloody thoughts, and Judas’s betraying thoughts, and the Pharisees’ cruel and blasphemous thoughts from afar. Oh, think this way: All these sinful thoughts defile and pollute the soul; they deface and spoil much of the inward beauty and glory of the soul. If I commit this or that sin to which my thoughts incline me, then either I must#footnote[Tears instead of gems were the ornaments of David’s bed when he had sinned, and so they must be yours, or else you must lie down in a bed of sorrow forever.] repent, or not repent. If I repent, it will cost me more grief, sorrow, shame, heartbreak, and soul-bleeding before my conscience is quieted, divine justice pacified, my comfort and joy restored, my evidences cleared, and my pardon sealed in the court of conscience, than the imagined profit or seeming sensual pleasure can be worth. “What fruit did you#super[+] reap at that time from the things of which you#super[+] are now ashamed?”

If I never repent, oh, then my sinful#footnote[Inward bleeding kills many a man; so will sinful thoughts, if they are not repented of.] thoughts will be the scorpions that will eternally vex me, the rods that will eternally lash me, the thorns that will everlastingly prick me, the dagger that will be eternally stabbing me, the worm that will be forever gnawing me. Oh, therefore watch against them, be constant in resisting them, and in lamenting and weeping over them, and then they will not hurt you, though they may trouble you for a time. And remember this: he who does this does more than the most glittering and blustering hypocrite in the world does.

The fifth #emph[remedy] against this device#footnote[Eph 3:19. The words are a Hebraism. When the Hebrews would set forth many excellent things, they add the name of God to it: city of God, cedars of God, wrestlings of God. So here, “that you#super[+] may be filled with the fullness of God.”] of Satan is to labor more and more to be filled with the fullness of God, and to be enriched with all spiritual and heavenly things. What is the reason that the angels in heaven do not have so much as an idle thought? It is because they are filled with the fullness of God. Take it as a truth proved by experience: the more the soul is filled with the fullness of God, and enriched with spiritual and heavenly things, the less room there is in that soul for vain thoughts. The fuller the vessel is of wine, the less room there is for water. Oh, lay up much of God, of Christ, of precious promises, and of choice experiences in your#super[+] hearts, and then you#super[+] will be less troubled with vain thoughts! “The good man brings good things out of the good treasure#footnote[Matt 12:35.] of his heart.”

The sixth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to keep up holy and spiritual affections, for as your affections are, so will your thoughts be. “Oh,#footnote[Ps 119:97. Ps 139:18.] how I love Your law! All day long it is my meditation.” What we love most, we muse on most. “When I awake, I am still with You.” What we greatly like, we will greatly think on. Those who are frequent in their love to God and his law will be frequent in thinking of God and his law. A child will not forget his mother.

The seventh #emph[remedy] against this device#footnote[2 Tim 2:4, ἐμπλέκεται, “is entangled.” It is a comparison that St. Paul borrows from the custom of the Roman Empire, in which soldiers were forbidden to act as agents in other men’s lawsuits, or to undertake farming or trade.] of Satan is to avoid a multiplicity of worldly business. Oh, do not let the world take up your#super[+] hearts and thoughts at other times! Souls that are torn in pieces with the cares of the world will always be vexed and tormented with vain thoughts in all their approaches to God. Vain thoughts will always be crowding in on him who lives in a crowd of business. The stars that have the smallest circuit are nearest the pole, and the men who are least entangled with business are commonly nearest to God.
